<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antiochus III the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/222-bce">222</a>–187 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Antiochus III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antiochus III the Great was a Seleucid king who reigned from 222 to 187 BCE. He restored Seleucid power in the east through his anabasis and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a> from the Ptolemies. His war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> ended in defeat and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> in 188 BCE, which imposed a heavy indemnity and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>'s decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great.jpg" alt="Antiochus III the Great" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_head_wearing_a_head-band_resembling_king_of_Syria_Antiochus_III_(223%E2%80%93187_BC),_late_1st_century_BC%E2%80%93early_1st_century_AD,_Louvre_Museum_(7462828632).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He cooperated with Rome against <b>Antiochus III</b> and rebuilt his kingdom's finances and manpower, but his reign ended in bitterness after he had his younger son Demetrius, a favourite of the Romans, executed in 180 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…with Rome, and according to Tacitus, envoys reminded the Senate in 53 CE of the help their city had given to Roman commanders in wars against the Macedonian kings, <b>Antiochus III</b>, Aristonicus, the pirates and Mithridates VI of Pontus; Claudius responded by remitting their tribute for five years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Hellenistic kings Chios remained a nominally free city, carefully balancing between rival dynasties and later cultivating friendship with Rome, which it supported against <b>Antiochus III</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome's victory over <b>Antiochus III</b> and his Aetolian allies in 191 BCE ended Aetolian control: the consul Manius Acilius Glabrio restored the sanctuary's property and the autonomy of the Delphians, which the Roman Senate soon confirmed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death at the Battle of Corupedium in 281 BCE the old name returned, and Ephesus passed between the Seleucid Empire and the Ptolemaic Empire until <b>Antiochus III</b> took it in 197–196 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Bactria seceded from the Seleucid Empire under Diodotus I around 250 BCE; Euthydemus, a Magnesian Greek, seized its throne about 230 BCE and withstood a three-year siege of Bactra by <b>Antiochus III</b>, who in about 206 BCE recognised him and offered a daughter to his son Demetrius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.19,48.26&amp;year=-205" aria-label="Susa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5791 993 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antiochus III the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6241 1263h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6241 1263h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.19,48.26&amp;year=-205">What was here in 205 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
